A||N||6||MickeyAsh||Questions/Preparations for A/C work||||5||
Z||000000||MickeyAsh||06-10-2001||01:15 AM||||Hey guys. I'm about to start working on the A/C system in the GT, as I live in Florida... and the windows being down just isnt cutting it anymore... here's what I know... hopefully one of you can tell me the rest.<BR>1) The A/C belt for the compressor has been off since before the previous owner had it, and he owned the car for 3 years, and I've had it for about 7 months... is the compressor probably dead, and how can I check?<P>2) The Compressor Hoses that are just in front of the engine are cracked open, and have been since I bought the car... what do I have to replace because of that? (besides the hoses  &gt;=) )<P>Any other little tips or ideas would be helpful... thanks in advance!<P>-Sheep||||reg||5
Z||000001||EdsB52||06-10-2001||02:26 AM||||Well, first I would establish whether I wanted to go with R-12 or R-134A. 12 is harder to come by, but works twice as well as 134. 134 is far cheaper, but peters out in high temp/high humidity conditions. The system you have now is R-12 I'm 99.999% sure. You can't mix the two types of freon. The system is probably flat right now, so it might have been contaminated. Anyway, if you want to do it right and have it last a long time, I would change the compressor, drier, and orifice tube (or whatever filtering device the Fiero uses), and all the O-rings. You can buy a kit of A/C O-rings for specific types of vehicles. And as far as the hose, I would go to NAPA or an auto A/C shop, bring my old hose, and have them refabricate a new one using the old ends. Then, when you get it put together, you have to decide which type of freon  to use. When you figure that out, you must buy the compatible type of oil and add the amount prescibed on the bottle into the system at the port specified. <P>You could try and cheap it, but if something is trashed, you'll spend more in the long run. If you're going to keep it, do it right. It will cost you about 400-500 to do it right by the time you get it serviced. So ultimately, decide how much you want ot spend. If you're going cheap, make sure to add oil to the compressor before firing.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||06-10-2001||11:47 AM||||Since your system is probably open and depleted, I'd go ahead and convert to R-134.  It's FAR cheaper than R-12.  I've converted several cars including my own Fiero and have no complaints with the way it cools, even on a hot, humid, Atlanta summer day <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000003||MickeyAsh||06-10-2001||12:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Rodrv6:<BR><B>Since your system is probably open and depleted, I'd go ahead and convert to R-134.  It's FAR cheaper than R-12.  I've converted several cars including my own Fiero and have no complaints with the way it cools, even on a hot, humid, Atlanta summer day  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah that has actually come to be my plan... I found the article at the fierowarehouse and I'm going to change over to 134a... as for the hoses, I'm probably going to take them to an A/C place and have them re-made with my old metal ends... I'm not up to blow $110 on some hoses... as for the compressor, I'm just going to yank it, and have it checked professionally (probably by the same person I have do my hoses)... I'll buy a new accumulator, install everything, and then pay someone to vacuum and charge the system... does that sound like a good plan?||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||06-10-2001||08:46 PM||||At this point you should consider the compressor shot...<P>Why...<P>If it's sat this long the shaft seal is probably failed or will soon after putting it in service. The shaft builds up corosion that chews out the seal.<P>The pulley probably has significant rust. That means the pulley profile has changed and you could have a fun time keeping belts on it. Sanding off the rust can make the problem worse. A wire brush on a grinder might do a better job but once the groove is out of profile you're pretty much done. You'll have high belt wear.<P>The clutch also has major rust and may not last long... Assuming it's springs and stuff are still good...<P>Getting this one ready for service will likely cost nearly as much as a rebuilt one.<P>The system needs to be pressure tested with nitrogen once the hoses are fixed. If the system has been open for a long time moisture that enters the openings can corrode the evaporator and condensor. the damage may not show up untill the system sees pressure.||||reg||1
Z||000005||MickeyAsh||06-10-2001||10:19 PM||||Just the kind of answer I was looking for, decision changing and more to the tone I had expected... so I'll go ahead and blow the cash on a new compressor, have a shop re-do my hoses, install a new accumulator, and then have it checked for leaks... ack... $269 for a condenser, $229 for an evaporator, $2-300 for a compressor... what fun... maybe cool air can wait a little while... if anyone knows some cheaper distributors for that hardware than the fiero store, lemme know... ill have to try PBoys and AZ... anywho... thanks again...<P>[This message has been edited by MickeyAsh (edited 06-10-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by MickeyAsh (edited 06-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||MrFeeArrow||06-11-2001||09:49 AM||||Just recently had mine converted over to 134a. Replaced everything except hoses and condensor. had it done by AllPro here in Jacksonville Florida. The bill came to $1037.66<P>Very happy and COOL with this conversion.<P>Anyone know anything about a replacement product for R12 called "Freeze 12"?||||reg||1
